Commit: ce7591cf34b9b851eef26e29674266693eaf31be

Hi @ijjk 
Thanks for your reply. 
I was aware, that the params used in the destination (like explained in https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/15626), are not added to the query as explained in the issue. 
However, I was not aware, that all params, even the unused ones, should be dropped as soon as one of them is used.

I still don't know how exactly I can access the param sector in the following example. We use the rewrite as sector is an optional parameter.
```
{
    async rewrites() {
      return [
        {
          source: "/:sector/product/:productid",
          destination: "/product/:productid",
        },
      ];
    }
  }
```

You can pass `sector` in the query manually by changing the above to:

```js
{
    async rewrites() {
      return [
        {
          source: "/:sector/product/:productid",
          destination: "/product/:productid?sector=:sector",
        },
      ];
    }
  }
```

I'm gonna close this PR for now as we aren't wanting to change the behavior currently and update the initial issue to mark it for documenting since it doesn't look like we mention this currently

Some ideas/thoughts about this approach:

- it allows for multiple image loads on the same site inherently
- it enables for other image loaders to be written in a separate code-splittable entry point and imported
 
E.g.

```js
// sanity-image.js
import { sanityImageLoader } from 'next/image-loaders';
import Image from 'next/image';

function SanityImage(props) {
  return <Image imageLoader={sanityImageLoader} {...props} />;
}

export default SanityImage;
```

```js
// contentful-image.js
import { contentfulImageLoader } from 'next/image-loaders';
import Image from 'next/image';

function ContentfulImage(props) {
  return <Image imageLoader={contentfulImageLoader} {...props} />;
}

export default ContentfulImage;
```

Yes, you are right about the styles in the style tag, but the styles will also be contained in the client bundle.

If you run `next build` and check out the bundle for the index page (for me `examples/with-emotion-vanilla/.next/static/chunks/pages/index-7bbaceef0f12af96f2f5.js`) and prettify it you will see code like this:

```js
function u() {
  var e = i([
    "\n  from, 20%, 53%, 80%, to {\n    transform: translate3d(0,0,0);\n  }\n\n  40%, 43% {\n    transform: translate3d(0, -30px, 0);\n  }\n\n  70% {\n    transform: translate3d(0, -15px, 0);\n  }\n\n  90% {\n    transform: translate3d(0,-4px,0);\n  }\n",
  ]);
  return (
    (u = function () {
      return e;
    }),
    e
  );
}
function s() {
  var e = i([
    "\n  font-size: 20px;\n  padding: 15px;\n  background: blue;\np  color: white;\n",
  ]);
  return (
    (s = function () {
      return e;
    }),
    e
  );
}
```

As you can see there is lots of whitespace in the strings that contain CSS. This increases the JS bundle size.

You should be able to minify the CSS in these strings by including [`@emotion/babel-plugin`](https://emotion.sh/docs/@emotion/babel-plugin) in a [custom babel config](https://nextjs.org/docs/advanced-features/customizing-babel-config).

