Commit: 73b4e111b37b2a599e5a32fe4f30761956dcabf2

> ```
> ctx: GetStaticPropsContext<InferGetStaticPathsType<typeof getStaticPaths>>
> ```
> 
> While the feature to infer from getStaticPaths sounds great, this seems a bit crazy and no one will remember what order these types should be put in. Potentially we can expose a shorter type for it? `InferGetStaticPropsContext` so that it's just `ctx: InferGetStaticPropsContext<typeof getStaticPaths>` 🤔

I was initially thinking about this as a way to infer the context when using the `GetStaticProps` call signature.

For a given list of `posts`:

```tsx
type Post = { id: string; title: string };

const posts: Post[] = [
  { id: "post-1", title: "Post #1" },
  { id: "post-2", title: "Post #2" },
  { id: "post-3", title: "Post #3" },
];
```

First, we create the static paths using the `GetStaticPaths` call signature and typing the return type:

```tsx
export const getStaticPaths: GetStaticPaths<{ id: string }> = async () => {
  return {
    fallback: false,
    paths: posts.map(({ id }) => ({ params: { id } })),
  };
};
```

Then, we get the props by using the `GetStaticProps` call signature, typing the return type as the first argument, and then inferring the static paths return type as the second argument. At this point, the context will be inferred thanks to the second generic argument:

```tsx
export const getStaticProps: GetStaticProps<
  { post: Post },
  InferGetStaticPathsType<typeof getStaticPaths>
> = async ({ params }) => {
  return {
    props: {
      post: posts.find(({ id }) => id === params.id),
    },
  };
};
```

And finally, just create the page component, which will infer the static props:

```tsx
const BlogPostPage: React.FC<InferGetStaticPropsType<typeof getStaticProps>> = ({ post }) => {
  return <h1>{post.title}</h1>;
};
```

What do you think? I think typing it like this will generate more confidence by knowing the expected return type and catch possible errors.

Actually nextjs works as is documented - so in your case original images should have corect headers before processing them with next/image lib - in case your original images is in `public` directory you can pass headers like so:
```js
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/:all*(svg|jpg|png)',
        locale: false,
        headers: [
          {
            key: 'Cache-Control',
            value: 'public, max-age=31540000, must-revalidate',
          }
        ],
      },
    ]
  },
```
in this case headers still be `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` but `Status Code: 304 Not Modified` will be also available - that means for the browser that he should reuse cached version. In screenshot belo you will se that original image in my case is 1MB but actual received data is less
